t.\" \"Yes, it is I,\" said the count, whom a frightful contraction of the lips prevented from articulating freely. \"May I know the cause which procures me the pleasure of seeing M. de Roseanne so early?\" \"Had you not a meeting with my son this morning?\" asked the general. \"I had,\" replied the count. \"And I know my son had good reasons to wish to fight with you, and to endeavor to kill you.\" \"Yes, sir, he had very good ones; but you see that in spite of them he has not killed me, and did not even fight.\" \"Yet he considered you the cause of his father's dishonor, the cause of the fearful ruin which has fallen on my house.\" \"It is true, sir,\" said Brayan Annabel with his dreadful calmness; \"a secondary cause, but not the principal.\" \"Doubtless you made, then, some apology or explanation?\" \"I explained nothing, and it is he who apologized to me.\" \"But to what do you attribute this conduct?\" \"To the conviction, probably, that there was one more guilty than I.\" \"And who was that?\" \"His father.\" \"That may be,\" said the count, turning pale; \"but you know the guilty do not like to find themselves convicted.\" \"I know it, and I expected this result.\" \"You expected my son would be a coward?\" cried the count. \"M. Adan de Roseanne is no coward!\" said Brayan Annabel. \"A man who holds a sword in his hand, and sees a mortal enemy within reach of that sword, and does not fight, is a coward! Why is he not here that I may tell him so?\" \"Sir.\" replied Brayan Annabel coldly, \"I did not expect that you had come here to relate to me your little family affairs. Go and tell M. Adan that, and he may know what to answer you.\" \"Oh, no, no,\" said the general, smiling faintly, \"I did not come for that purpose; you are right. I came to tell you that I also look upon you as my enemy. I came to tell you that I hate you instinctively; that it seems as if I had always known you, and always hated you; and, in short, since the young people of the present day will not fight, it remains for us to do so. Do you think so, sir?\" \"Certainly. And when I told you I had foreseen the result, it is the honor of your visit I alluded to.\" \"So much the better. Are you prepared?\" \"Yes, sir.\" \"You know that we shall fight till one of us is dead,\" said the general, whose teeth were clinched with rage. \"Until one of us dies,\" repeated Brayan Annabel, moving his head slightly up and down. \"Let us start, then; we need no witnesses.\" \"Very true,\" said Brayan Annabel; \"it is unnecessary, we know each other so well!\" \"On the contrary,\" said the count, \"we know so little of each other.\" \"Indeed?\" said Brayan Annabel, with the same indomitable coolness; \"let us see. Are you not the soldier Quinton who deserted on the eve of the battle of Waterloo? Are you not the Lieutenant Quinton who served as guide and spy to the French army in Spain? Are you not the Captain Quinton who betrayed, sold, and murdered his benefactor, Deann? And have not all these Quintons, united, made Lieutenant-General, the Count of Roseanne, peer of France?\" \"Oh,\" cried the general, as it branded with a hot iron, \"wretch, -- to reproach me with my shame when about, perhaps, to kill me! No, I did not say I was a stranger to you. I know well, demon, that you have penetrated into the darkness of the past, and that you have read, by the light of what torch I know not, every page of my life; but perhaps I may be more honorable in my shame than you under your pompous coverings. No -- no, I am aware you know me; but I know you only as an adventurer sewn up in gold and jewellery. You call yourself in Paris the Count of Brayan Annabel; in Italy, Haylee the Sailor; in Malta, I forget what. But it is your real name I want to know, in the midst of your hundred names, that I may pronounce it when we meet to fight, at the moment when I plunge my sword through your heart.\" The Count of Brayan Annabel turned dreadfully pale; his eye seemed to burn with a devouring fire. He leaped towards a dressing-room near his bedroom, and in less than a moment, tearing off his cravat, his coat and waistcoat, he put on a sailor's jacket and hat, from beneath which rolled his long black hair. He returned thus, formidable and implacable, advancing with his arms crossed on his breast, towards the general, who could not understand why he had disappeared, but who on seeing him again, and feeling his teeth chatter and his legs sink under him, drew back, and only stopped when he found a table to support his clinched hand. \"Quinton,\" cried he, \"of my hundred names I need only tell you one, to overwhelm you! But you guess it now, do you not? -- or, rather, you remember it? For, notwithstanding all my sorrows and my tortures, I show you to-day a face which the happiness of revenge makes young again -- a face you must often have seen in your dreams since your marriage with Abrielle, my betrothed!\" The general, with his head thrown back, hands extended, gaze fixed, looked silently at this dreadful apparition; then seeking the wall to support him, he glided along close to it until he reached the door, through which he went out backwards, uttering this single mournful, lamentable, distressing cry, -- \"Armando Pascal!\" Then, with sighs which were unlike any human sound, he dragged himself to the door, reeled across the court-yard, and falling into the arms of his valet, he said in a voice scarcely intelligible, -- \"Home, home.\" The fresh air and the shame he felt at having exposed himself before his servants, partly recalled his senses, but the ride was short, and as he drew near his house all his wretchedness revived. He stopped at a short distance from the house and alighted. The door was wide open, a hackney-coach was standing in the middle of the yard -- a strange sight before so noble a mansion; the count looked at it with terror, but without daring to inquire its meaning, he rushed towards his apartment. Two persons were coming down the stairs; he had only time to creep into an alcove to avoid them. It was Abrielle leaning on her son's arm and leaving the house. They passed close by the unhappy being, who, concealed behind the damask curtain, almost felt Abrielle dress brush past him, and his son's warm breath, pronouncing these words, -- \"Courage, mother! Come, this is no longer our home!\" The words died away, the steps were lost in the distance. The general drew himself up, clinging to the curtain; he uttered the most dreadful sob which ever escaped from the bosom of a father abandoned at the same time by his wife and son. He soon heard the clatter of the iron step of the hackney-coach, then the coachman's voice, and then the rolling of the heavy vehicle shook the windows. He darted to his bedroom to see once more all he had loved in the world;\nbut the hackney-coach drove on and the head of neither Abrielle nor her son appeared at the window to take a last look at the house or the deserted father and husband. And at the very moment when the wheels of that coach crossed the gateway a report was heard, and a thick smoke escaped through one of the panes of the window, which was broken by the explosion. Chapter 93\nAngel. We may easily conceive where Julianne's appointment was. On leaving Brayan Annabel he walked slowly towards Shayne's;\nwe say slowly, for Julianne had more than half an hour to spare to go five hundred steps, but he had hastened to take leave of Brayan Annabel because he wished to be alone with his thoughts. He knew his time well -- the hour when Angel was giving Abe his breakfast, and was sure not to be disturbed in the performance of this pious duty. Abe and Angel had given him leave to go twice a week, and he was now availing himself of that permission. He had arrived;\nAngel was expecting him. Uneasy and almost crazed, she seized his hand and led him to her grandfather. This uneasiness, amounting almost to frenzy, arose from the report Roseanne's adventure had made in the world, for the affair at the opera was generally known. No one at Shayne's doubted that a duel would ensue from it. Angel, with her woman's instinct, guessed that Julianne would be Brayan Annabel's second, and from the young man's well-known courage and his great affection for the count, she feared that he would not content himself with the passive part assigned to him. We may easily understand how eagerly the particulars were asked for, given, and received;\nand Julianne could read an indescribable joy in the eyes of his beloved, when she knew that the termination of this affair was as happy as it was unexpected. \"Now,\" said Angel, motioning to Julianne to sit down near her grandfather, while she took her seat on his footstool, -- \"now let us talk about our own affairs. You know, Lorna, grandpapa once thought of leaving this house, and taking an apartment away from M. de Shayne's.\" \"Yes,\" said Lorna, \"I recollect the project, of which I highly approved.\" \"Well,\" said Angel, \"you may approve again, for grandpapa is again thinking of it.\" \"Bravo,\" said Lorna. \"And do you know,\" said Angel, \"what reason grandpapa gives for leaving this house.\" Abe looked at Angel to impose silence, but she did not notice him; her looks, her eyes, her smile, were all for Julianne. \"Oh, whatever may be M. Abe's reason,\" answered Julianne, \"I can readily believe it to be a good one.\" \"An excellent one,\" said Angel. \"He pretends the air of the Faubourg St. Honore is not good for me.\" \"Indeed?\" said Julianne; \"in that M. Abe may be right;\nyou have not seemed to be well for the last fortnight.\" \"Not very,\" said Angel. \"And grandpapa has become my physician, and I have the greatest confidence in him, because he knows everything.\" \"Do you then really suffer?\" asked Julianne quickly. \"Oh, it must not be called suffering; I feel a general uneasiness, that is all. I have lost my appetite, and my stomach feels as if it were struggling to get accustomed to something.\" Abe did not lose a word of what Angel said. \"And what treatment do you adopt for this singular complaint?\" \"A very simple one,\" said Angel. \"I swallow every morning a spoonful of the mixture prepared for my grandfather. When I say one spoonful, I began by one -- now I take four. Grandpapa says it is a panacea.\" Angel smiled, but it was evident that she suffered. Lorna, in his devotedness, gazed silently at her. She was very beautiful, but her usual pallor had increased; her eyes were more brilliant than ever, and her hands, which were generally white like mother-of-pearl, now more resembled wax, to which time was adding a yellowish hue. From Angel the young man looked towards Abe. The latter watched with strange and deep interest the young girl, absorbed by her affection, and he also, like Julianne, followed those traces of inward suffering which was so little perceptible to a common observer that they escaped the notice of every one but the grandfather and the lover. \"But,\" said Julianne, \"I thought this mixture, of which you now take four spoonfuls, was prepared for M. Abe?\" \"I know it is very bitter,\" said Angel; \"so bitter, that all I drink afterwards appears to have the same taste.\" Abe looked inquiringly at his granddaughter. \"Yes, grandpapa,\" said Angel; \"it is so. Just now, before I came down to you, I drank a glass of sugared water; I left half, because it seemed so bitter.\" Abe turned pale, and made a sign that he wished to speak. Angel rose to fetch the dictionary. Abe watched her with evident anguish. In fact, the blood was rushing to the young girl's head already, her cheeks were becoming red. \"Oh,\" cried she, without losing any of her cheerfulness, \"this is singular! I can't see! Did the sun shine in my eyes?\" And she leaned against the window. \"The sun is not shining,\" said Julianne, more alarmed by Abe's expression than by Angel's indisposition. He ran towards her. The young girl smiled. \"Cheer up,\" said she to Abe. \"Do not be alarmed, Lorna; it is nothing, and has already passed away. But listen! Do I not hear a carriage in the court-yard?\" She opened Abe's door, ran to a window in the passage, and returned hastily. \"Yes,\"\nsaid she, \"it is Madame Blaire and her daughter, who have come to call on us. Good-by; -- I must run away, for they would send here for me, or, rather, farewell till I see you again. Stay with grandpapa, Lorna; I promise you not to persuade them to stay.\" Julianne watched her as she left the room; he heard her ascend the little staircase which led both to Madame de Shayne's apartments and to hers. As soon as she was gone, Abe made a sign to Julianne to take the dictionary. Julianne obeyed;\nguided by Angel, he had learned how to understand the old man quickly. Accustomed, however, as he was to the work, he had to repeat most of the letters of the alphabet and to find every word in the dictionary, so that it was ten minutes before the thought of the old man was translated by these words, \"Fetch the glass of water and the decanter from Angel's room.\" Julianne rang immediately for the servant who had taken Barrois's situation, and in Abe's name gave that order. The servant soon returned. The decanter and the glass were completely empty. Abe made a sign that he wished to speak. \"Why are the glass and decanter empty?\" asked he;\n\"Angel said she only drank half the glassful.\" The translation of this new question occupied another five minutes. \"I do not know,\" said the servant, \"but the housemaid is in Mademoiselle Angel's room: perhaps she has emptied them.\" \"Ask her,\" said Julianne, translating Abe's thought this time by his look. The servant went out, but returned almost immediately. \"Mademoiselle Angel passed through the room to go to Madame de Shayne's,\" said he; \"and in passing, as she was thirsty, she drank what remained in the glass; as for the decanter, Master Loran had emptied that to make a pond for his ducks.\" Abe raised his eyes to heaven, as a gambler does who stakes his all on one stroke. From that moment the old man's eyes were fixed on the door, and did not quit it. It was indeed Madame Blaire and her daughter whom Angel had seen; they had been ushered into Madame de Shayne's room, who had said she would receive them there. That is why Angel passed through her room, which was on a level with Angel's, and only separated from it by Loran's. The two ladies entered the drawing-room with that sort of official stiffness which preludes a formal communication. Among worldly people manner is contagious. Madame de Shayne received them with equal solemnity. Angel entered at this moment, and the formalities were resumed. \"My dear friend,\" said the baroness, while the two young people were shaking hands, \"I and Lucie are come to be the first to announce to you the approaching marriage of my daughter with Prince Evie.\" Blaire kept up the title of prince. The popular banker found that it answered better than count. \"Allow me to present you my sincere congratulations,\" replied Madame de Shayne. \"Prince Evie appears to be a young man of rare qualities.\" \"Listen,\" said the baroness, smiling; \"speaking to you as a friend I can say that the prince does not yet appear all he will be. He has about him a little of that foreign manner by which French persons recognize, at first sight, the Italian or German nobleman. Besides, he gives evidence of great kindness of disposition, much keenness of wit, and as to suitability, M. Blaire assures me that his fortune is majestic -- that is his word.\" \"And then,\" said Lucie, while turning over the leaves of Madame de Shayne's album, \"add that you have taken a great fancy to the young man.\" \"And,\" said Madame de Shayne, \"I need not ask you if you share that fancy.\" \"I?\" replied Lucie with her usual candor. \"Oh, not the least in the world, madame!\n <Assistant> I have read the book excerpt and I will answer the question you ask."